--- CHAPTER 10: HOW WE USED AI CRITICALLY ---
Three hotspots in a calm "reflection" scene.
10.1 [lightbulb icon] "The initial insight" | DETAIL: "When analysing all the sources across our four disciplines, NotebookLM helped us find a connection between technical design and psychological adherence. We cross-referenced an analysis showing that sustained engagement is essential for cognitive training effectiveness with studies stating that older adults require extra time and aid to operate digital technologies; the AI also found that poor UI/UX design negatively affects rehabilitation, as it creates cognitive barriers for users, which causes them to be demotivated. Without NotebookLM identifying this interaction, we might view usability and adherence as separate entities, without realising that senior-friendly design is a prerequisite for rehabilitation effectiveness."
10.2 [warning icon] "The system failure" | DETAIL: "We encountered two main problems when using Gemini. First, although we had imported only one source, Gemini began discussing other studies listed in that paper's references. This made it unclear which findings came from the uploaded paper and which came from other studies, so we could not assume the extensions were reliable or accurate simply because Gemini mentioned them. Second, when we used NotebookLM in the Gemini Enterprise environment to analyse local policies and healthcare guidelines, some responses produced an irrelevant list of internal citations instead of an explanation of the documents, and the system rejected the website URLs we tried to import, including local MOH resources and the AIHGle 2.0 guidelines. Without these sources, the analysis could miss important information about Singapore's healthcare policies and responsibilities, leaving a general discussion of technology that did not sufficiently consider its use in Singapore."
10.3 [compass icon] "The strategic intervention" | DETAIL: "We addressed the technical problems by switching from the Google enterprise tool to a standard Google NotebookLM workspace, which resolved the citation error. We also downloaded relevant information from the MOH websites, saved it as PDFs and uploaded them, adding the policy material that the website imports had failed to include. Next, we asked NotebookLM to examine the problem from government and business perspectives, specifically considering the costs of dementia described in the National Academies publication together with the AIHGle 2.0 guidelines. Uploading more sources was only part of the process; we also identified what was lacking and asked questions that directed the AI towards those areas. However, a clearer and more detailed response did not necessarily mean every claim was correct. In future, we would ask it to separate the uploaded paper's findings from references it quoted from other studies, and we would obtain and check any such studies separately. We remained responsible for deciding whether the AI's conclusions were supported by the sources, by manually checking and validating them."

